---
import ToolLayout from '../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import FAQ from '../components/FAQ.astro';
import RelatedTools from '../components/RelatedTools.astro';
import { toolBySlug } from '../data/tools';

const tool = toolBySlug['auto-loan-calculator'];
const breadcrumbItems = [{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: tool.title }];
const jsonLd = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    "headline": "Auto Financing: New vs Used Car Loans & Free Calculator",
    "description": "Compare new and used car financing options, understand APR differences, and calculate your monthly auto loan payment. Free auto loan calculator included.",
    "author": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "FreeOnlineToolkit" },
    "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "FreeOnlineToolkit" },
    "datePublished": "2026-07-26",
    "dateModified": "2026-07-26",
    "mainEntityOfPage": { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": "https://freeonlinetoolkit.org/auto-loan-calculator/" }
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "HowTo",
    "name": "How to Calculate Auto Loan Payments",
    "description": "Calculate monthly auto loan payments and compare financing options.",
    "step": [
      { "@type": "HowToStep", "text": "Enter the vehicle price" },
      { "@type": "HowToStep", "text": "Input your down payment and trade-in value" },
      { "@type": "HowToStep", "text": "Enter the interest rate and loan term" },
      { "@type": "HowToStep", "text": "Click Calculate to see your monthly payment" },
    ],
  },
];
---

<ToolLayout title="Auto Financing: New vs Used Car Loans & Free Calculator | FreeOnlineToolkit" description="Compare new and used car financing options, understand APR differences, and calculate your monthly auto loan payment. Free auto loan calculator included." breadcrumbItems={breadcrumbItems} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <fragment slot="heading"><h1>Auto Financing: New vs Used Car Loans & Free Calculator</h1></fragment>

  <div class="key-takeaways" style="background:#eff6ff;border:1px solid #93c5fd;border-radius:8px;padding:1.25rem;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
    <h3 style="margin-top:0;color:#1e40af;">Key Takeaways</h3>
    <ul style="margin-bottom:0;">
      <li>New car loans typically offer lower interest rates (0–6.9% APR) than used car loans (4–14% APR), but the higher purchase price often offsets the rate advantage.</li>
      <li>A 2–3 year old used car offers the best value — the steepest depreciation has passed, and the car may still have warranty coverage remaining.</li>
      <li>The "20/4/10 rule" is a smart financing guideline: 20% down, 4-year maximum term, and total transportation costs under 10% of gross income.</li>
      <li>Dealer financing can be competitive, especially for manufacturer-subsidised 0% APR deals, but always compare with bank and credit union offers.</li>
      <li>Leasing vs buying: leasing offers lower monthly payments but you build no equity — buying is almost always cheaper over the long term.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>When it comes to buying a vehicle, one of the biggest decisions isn't which car to choose — it's how to pay for it. The financing structure you select affects your monthly budget, your total cost over time, and even your financial flexibility for years to come. With the average new car transaction price now exceeding $48,000 and average monthly payments over $700, getting the financing right is more important than ever. The good news is that a little knowledge goes a long way toward saving thousands of dollars.</p>

  <p>The first major decision is <strong>new vs used</strong>. New cars come with the latest technology, full manufacturer warranties, and often promotional financing rates as low as 0% APR. However, they lose 20–35% of their value in the first three years alone. A used car that's 2–3 years old has already absorbed that depreciation hit, meaning you're paying significantly less for a vehicle that's still relatively modern and likely still under warranty. The trade-off is that used car interest rates are typically 0.5–2% higher than new car rates, and you may have less choice in colour, features, and trim level.</p>

  <p>The <strong>20/4/10 rule</strong> is a widely recommended framework for auto financing: put down at least 20% of the purchase price, finance for no more than 4 years (48 months), and keep total transportation costs (payment, insurance, fuel, maintenance) below 10% of your gross monthly income. This guideline helps prevent the common trap of buying more car than you can afford. For example, on a $50,000 salary ($4,167/month gross), your total car costs should stay under $417/month — which means a car payment of roughly $250–300 after insurance and fuel costs.</p>

  <p>Understanding the <strong>true cost of financing</strong> is crucial. A $35,000 car financed at 6.9% over 72 months costs $39,380 total — that's $4,380 in interest alone. The same car financed over 36 months at 5.9% costs $37,280, saving over $2,100. And if you can put $7,000 down (20%) instead of $5,000, you save another $1,400 in interest. These numbers add up quickly, which is why financial advisors consistently recommend larger down payments and shorter loan terms, even if the monthly payment is higher.</p>

  <fragment slot="intro">
    <p class="tool-intro">Use the free auto loan calculator below to estimate your monthly payment and compare different financing scenarios. Adjust the down payment, rate, and term to find the best option for your budget.</p>
  </fragment>

  <fragment slot="calculator">
    <div class="calc-card">
      <h2>Auto Loan Calculator</h2>
      <div class="form-group">
        <label for="autoPrice">Vehicle Price ($)</label>
        <input type="number" id="autoPrice" value="35000" min="0" step="1000">
      </div>
      <div class="form-row">
        <div class="form-group">
          <label for="autoDown">Down Payment ($)</label>
          <input type="number" id="autoDown" value="5000" min="0" step="500">
        </div>
        <div class="form-group">
          <label for="autoRate">Annual Interest Rate (%)</label>
          <input type="number" id="autoRate" value="6.9" min="0" step="0.1">
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="form-group">
        <label for="autoTerm">Loan Term (Months)</label>
        <select id="autoTerm">
          <option value="36">36 months (3 years)</option>
          <option value="48">48 months (4 years)</option>
          <option value="60" selected>60 months (5 years)</option>
          <option value="72">72 months (6 years)</option>
          <option value="84">84 months (7 years)</option>
        </select>
      </div>
      <div class="btn-group">
        <button class="btn btn-primary" id="calcBtn" data-i18n="common.calculate">Calculate</button>
        <button class="btn btn-secondary" id="resetBtn" data-i18n="common.reset">Reset</button>
      </div>

      <div class="result-box" id="autoResult">
        <div class="result-value" id="autoPayment">$0.00</div>
        <div class="result-label">Monthly Payment</div>
        <div class="result-details">
          <div><span class="label">Loan Amount</span><span class="value" id="autoLoanAmt">$0</span></div>
          <div><span class="label">Total Interest</span><span class="value" id="autoInterest">$0</span></div>
          <div><span class="label">Total Cost</span><span class="value" id="autoTotal">$0</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </fragment>

  <fragment slot="content">
    <div class="content-section">
      <h2>What This Tool Shows</h2>
      <p>This calculator applies the standard amortisation formula to your vehicle price, down payment, rate, and term. Use it to compare scenarios side by side — try a 48-month term vs 72 months, or $5,000 down vs $10,000, to see how each choice affects your monthly payment and total cost.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="content-section">
      <h2>Auto Loan Formula</h2>
      <div class="formula-box">M = P × [r(1+r)^n] / [(1+r)^n - 1]</div>
      <p>Where <strong>M</strong> = monthly payment, <strong>P</strong> = loan amount, <strong>r</strong> = monthly interest rate (annual rate ÷ 12), <strong>n</strong> = total months. This produces a fixed monthly payment where each payment reduces the principal while covering accrued interest.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="content-section">
      <h2>Leasing vs Buying: Which Is Right for You?</h2>
      <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:1rem 0;">
        <thead>
          <tr style="background:#f1f5f9;">
            <th style="padding:10px;text-align:left;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;">Factor</th>
            <th style="padding:10px;text-align:left;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;">Buying</th>
            <th style="padding:10px;text-align:left;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;">Leasing</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;">Monthly payment</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;">Higher</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;">Lower (you pay depreciation only)</td></tr>
          <tr style="background:#eff6ff;"><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;">Ownership</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;">You own the car</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;">You return it at lease end</td></tr>
          <tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;">Mileage limits</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;">Unlimited</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;">Typically 10–15K miles/year</td></tr>
          <tr style="background:#eff6ff;"><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;">Long-term cost</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;">Cheaper (you keep the car)</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;">More expensive (perpetual payments)</td></tr>
          <tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;">Best for</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;">Long-term ownership, high mileage</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;">Wanting a new car every 2–3 years</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>The bottom line: buying is almost always cheaper over the long term. Leasing makes sense only if you genuinely want a new car every 2–3 years and drive within mileage limits.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="content-section">
      <h2>How to Get the Best Auto Loan Rate</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Check your credit score first:</strong> Know where you stand before applying. Dispute any errors on your credit report.</li>
        <li><strong>Get pre-approved by 2–3 lenders:</strong> Banks, credit unions, and online lenders. Multiple auto loan inquiries within a 14-day window count as a single hard inquiry.</li>
        <li><strong>Consider credit unions:</strong> They typically offer rates 1–2% lower than traditional banks.</li>
        <li><strong>Negotiate the car price separately from financing:</strong> Agree on the out-the-door price first, then discuss financing.</li>
        <li><strong>Watch for manufacturer incentives:</strong> 0% APR deals can save thousands, but read the fine print — they sometimes require shorter terms or exclude certain models.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <FAQ items={[
      { question: "What is a good APR for an auto loan?", answer: "Rates vary by credit score. Excellent credit (740+) can get 4-6%, good credit (680-739) 6-9%, and fair credit (620-679) 9-14% on new cars." },
      { question: "How much should I put down on a car?", answer: "A down payment of 20% is recommended to avoid being upside down on your loan. Even 10% can make a significant difference in your monthly payment." },
      { question: "Is it better to finance through a dealer or bank?", answer: "Credit unions typically offer the best rates. Dealers may match or beat bank rates, especially for manufacturer-subsidised deals. Always get pre-approved first so you have a baseline to compare." },
      { question: "Should I pay off my car loan early?", answer: "If your loan has no prepayment penalty and the interest rate is higher than what you'd earn investing the money, paying early saves on interest. Check your loan agreement for prepayment terms." },
    ]} />

    <div class="content-section">
      <h2>Next Steps</h2>
      <p>Use the calculator above to model your ideal financing scenario. Before visiting a dealer, check your credit score, get pre-approved by at least one lender, and research the fair market value of your target vehicle on <a href="https://www.kbb.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kelley Blue Book</a> or <a href="https://www.edmunds.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Edmunds</a>. Remember: the goal isn't the lowest monthly payment — it's the lowest total cost while staying within your budget.</p>
    </div>
  </fragment>

  <fragment slot="sidebar">
    <RelatedTools currentSlug="auto-loan-calculator" category="financial" />
  </fragment>
</ToolLayout>

<script>
import { getNum, formatCurrency, showResult, hideResult } from '../utils/calculator-utils';

function calcAuto() {
  const price = getNum('autoPrice')!;
  const down = getNum('autoDown') || 0;
  const rate = getNum('autoRate')!;
  const term = parseInt((document.getElementById('autoTerm') as HTMLSelectElement).value);

  if (!price || !rate) return;
  const loanAmt = Math.max(0, price - down);
  if (loanAmt <= 0) return;

  const r = rate / 100 / 12;
  const M = loanAmt * (r * Math.pow(1 + r, term)) / (Math.pow(1 + r, term) - 1);
  const totalInt = M * term - loanAmt;

  document.getElementById('autoPayment')!.textContent = formatCurrency(M);
  document.getElementById('autoLoanAmt')!.textContent = formatCurrency(loanAmt);
  document.getElementById('autoInterest')!.textContent = formatCurrency(totalInt);
  document.getElementById('autoTotal')!.textContent = formatCurrency(price + totalInt);
  showResult('autoResult');
}

function resetAuto() {
  (document.getElementById('autoPrice') as HTMLInputElement).value = '35000';
  (document.getElementById('autoDown') as HTMLInputElement).value = '5000';
  (document.getElementById('autoRate') as HTMLInputElement).value = '6.9';
  (document.getElementById('autoTerm') as HTMLSelectElement).value = '60';
  hideResult('autoResult');
}

document.getElementById('calcBtn')!.addEventListener('click', calcAuto);
document.getElementById('resetBtn')!.addEventListener('click', resetAuto);
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', calcAuto);
</script>
